---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Licensing"
  description="The OpenE2EE Signal Protocol SDK is available under AGPLv3, or under a commercial license for proprietary products."
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="container measure">
      <p class="label">Licensing</p>
      {/*
        The heading names the two paths instead of counting them. A reader
        arrives here from four different link labels and needs to see the two
        licence names, which is how Qt's licensing page leads.

        The lead said three things and this page said all three again below:
        "the same code either way" is /pricing's Evaluating row, "what differs
        is the obligations" is what the two rows under this are, and "a summary,
        not the license itself" is stated twice more on this 250-word page.
        Elastic's licence FAQ and MongoDB's SSPL FAQ each state that disclaimer
        once, at the top.
      */}
      <h1>AGPLv3, or a commercial license.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        This page summarizes the two paths. The license text and your agreement control.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container">
      <dl class="definition-rows">
        <div>
          <dt>AGPLv3</dt>
          <dd>
            If you offer your product over a network, the AGPLv3 source obligations apply to it.
            Read the license text before you rely on this path.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Commercial license</dt>
          <dd>
            A separately negotiated production grant for proprietary products, so AGPLv3 does not
            apply to the covered product. Scoped to a named legal entity, named covered products,
            and an explicit licensed-component list.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band-surface">
    <div class="container measure prose">
      {/*
        Three paragraphs, 111 words, down to one. The first was the only one
        carrying a fact this page owns, and even it enumerated the five governed
        items that /pricing's fine print and /legal/terms §2 both print already.
        /pricing owns the enumeration because it sits next to the money, so this
        page keeps only the precedence rule.

        The second paragraph said which instrument applies per tier, which is
        /pricing's "How buying works" rows word for word. The third closed on
        "It is a shorter conversation than the one after launch", and its action
        is the link directly below it.

        The AGPLv3 row above now names the license text as its destination, so
        the LICENSE link is the one this row keeps and "Review Startup terms" is
        the one it drops; /pricing carries that one twice. It is labelled
        "LICENSE" rather than "Read the license text" because /security reaches
        the same file and one destination gets one name.
      */}
      <h2>The agreement is the license</h2>
      <p>
        This website describes the commercial model. It is not legal advice.
        Where this page and your <a href="/legal/terms">agreement</a> differ, the agreement
        controls.
      </p>
      <p class="text-links">
        <a href="/pricing">Pricing</a>
        <a href="mailto:licensing@open-e2ee.dev?subject=Commercial%20licensing%20question">
          Ask a licensing question
        </a>
        <a href="https://github.com/open-e2ee/signal-protocol-js/blob/main/LICENSE">
          LICENSE
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
